WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.589
By all accounts, this beloved Harry Potter
veteran would like to continue acting, but

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.460
there are a few factors that might be making
directors a bit hesitant to work with him

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.100
these days.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.840
Here's why Robbie Coltrane isn't landing the
high-profile roles he used to, and how he

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:14.840
could turn it around.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.340
Many creative professionals struggle with
substance abuse, and it seems to be especially

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.420
common in Hollywood.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.460
No matter how successful someone looks on
the surface, there's no guarantee that they

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.180
aren't struggling behind the scenes.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.140
Like many other actors, Coltrane has had a
troubled relationship with alcohol.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.060
The Guardian quoted him as saying,

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.500
"Booze is my undoing.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.640
I can drink a gallon of beer and not feel
the least bit drunk."

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.160
Coltrane isn't the only former Harry Potter
cast member who has a history with alcohol.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.160
Daniel Radcliffe has also owned up to drinking
heavily during those early years of his career,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.160
and he's said he was even under the influence
while shooting some scenes in the films.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.600
While Coltrane may be far from alone in this
struggle, it's still likely had an impact

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.400
on his ability to land new roles.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.780
"Why do you drink so much?"

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.060
"I like it!"

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.780
Between long days on set, physically demanding
scenes, and the lack of sleep that comes with

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.480
a long schedule, acting can wear a person
down.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:12.440
Dealing with health problems can really set
an actor back, and unfortunately, Coltrane

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.060
has been in poor health for years.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.420
He suffers from osteoarthritis, which has
only been getting worse with age.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:23.320
Chronic pain caused by the condition has become
so severe that he spends most of his days

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.040
in a wheelchair.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.080
He's planning to get a knee replacement at
some point in the future, and his doctors

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.920
have been encouraging him to lose weight to
make it possible.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.600
Coltrane is also diabetic.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.960
Hopefully, his knee replacement will alleviate
some of the pain and help him get back in

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:37.960
front of the camera.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.860
After his Potter years came to an end, Coltrane
continued working on a variety of films and

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:43.480
TV shows.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.390
Unfortunately, none have received the same
kind of adoration that the Wizarding World

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:48.880
has.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.680
It's unlikely that Coltrane expected to experience
that same level of acclaim again, but it's

00:01:53.681 --> 00:01:58.210
probably frustrating to star in such a popular
series and then appear in films that don't

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.360
go over as well with audiences and critics.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:06.600
In 2013, Coltrane played Jaggers in an adaptation
of Charles Dickens' Great Expectations.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.750
Although it was based on a classic story,
and Coltrane's performance was generally well

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.920
regarded, the film itself got a mixed critical
response.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:19.540
In 2015, he played the doctor in Effie Gray,
a film about a love triangle in the Victorian-era

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.040
art world.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.020
It too was met with a mixed response.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.900
For a successful actor like Coltrane, a string
of mediocre films can probably make him feel

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.880
like he has to prove himself all over again.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.140
Some of Coltrane's post-Potter roles have
been far from family-friendly.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.870
For example, he took a risk by starring in
the British Channel 4 TV series National Treasure

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:41.960
as a fictional comedian accused of assaulting
a teenage girl.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.820
It focuses on the pitfalls of trial-by-media
and how it affects legal proceedings.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.000
Coltrane was enthusiastic about the project
from the beginning, but people warned him

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.070
that playing such a controversial character,
especially with similar current issues in

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.980
the entertainment industry, might not be the
best idea.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.120
Coltrane decided to go for it anyway.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.560
As he told Channel 4,

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.440
"A lot of people said, 'Don't touch it, Rob,
it's poisonous, it's not going to do you any

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:06.940
good.'"

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.420
His response to those objections was the sensible
reminder, "It's pretend!"

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:16.580
Still, it was a 180 from his previous roles,
and casting directors might be wondering what

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.380
to do with him.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.660
Coltrane hasn't been completely out of work.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.380
Since 2016, he's hosted the series Robbie
Coltrane's Critical Evidence, in which he

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.310
explores some of the strangest and most confounding
murder cases in Britain.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.340
There's no question that true crime stories
are intriguing for many viewers, and the show

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.700
definitely has an audience, as it's run for
two seasons so far.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.460
Critical Evidence has been Coltrane's main
project for the past two years.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.960
It truly seems like a passion project for
him.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.200
Judging by some of the roles he's taken on
over the years, like his character in his

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:50.940
breakout work on the '90s TV series Cracker,
he's clearly interested in the psychology

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:51.760
of criminals.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.820
For now, Critical Evidence has taken precedence
over other potential jobs.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.020
"The difference between success and failure?

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.020
Critical evidence."

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.420
Despite his health problems, Coltrane is not
quite ready to hang up his hat just yet.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.340
In 2018, his agent, Belinda Wright, told Express
that Coltrane was slated to appear in an in-development

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.680
film and a TV series.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:16.019
However, since his health issues have worsened
recently, these projects may have been put

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:16.860
on the back burner.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.340
At the moment, it's unlikely that Coltrane
would be able to put in long hours on set

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.580
due to his chronic pain.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.000
A source told Express that Coltrane wants
to end his career in TV with one final award-winning

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.650
performance, and ideally, he'll get that chance
after his knee operation.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.620
Hopefully, his surgery will be a success,
and he'll be able to return to doing what

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:36.540
he loves most.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.620
His fans definitely miss his presence on the
big screen.

